    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §80-b, Hway L (as proposed in S.3897 & A.8936-A); amd §15, Chap 329 of 1991",
    "lawSection": "Highway Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S840\n\nSPONSOR: KENNEDY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the highway law, in relation to the percentage responsi-\nbility of the state for federally assisted projects; and to amend chap-\nter 329 of the laws of 1991 amending the state finance law and other\nlaws relating to establishing the dedicated highway and bridge trust\nfund and the dedicated mass transportation fund, in relation to the\nstate share of municipal projects where the municipality funds a\ncomplete street design\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nMakes a chapter amendment to a 2022 law providing for a higher level of\nstate reimbursement for local use of complete street design features on\nfederally aided projects.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis chapter amendment modifies the section of law for which the\nincreased reimbursement rate would apply; it does not change the fund\nsource being used to provide the increased match.\n\nThe chapter amendment also clarifies that the State Department of Trans-\nportation (DOT) is empowered to determine that a local project suffi-\nciently complies with the State's Complete Streets Law before providing\nthe increased match. DOT is required to make guidance publicly available\nfor local applicants to use.\n\nThe chapter amendment preserves the original intent of the bill, which\nreduces a municipality's percentage of responsibility from five percent\nto two and one-half percent of the total project cost on a federally\naided highway project that uses complete streets design features.\n\nSection 3 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent federal complete street regulations largely exempt most highway\nproject in New York, because reviews for the incorporation of complete\nstreets features (such as pedestrian crosswalks, sidewalks, and bike\nlanes) are only required for large, intensive new construction projects.\nSince New York and many states in the Northeast already have a built-out\nnetwork of highways and roads, complete street reviews are conducted\nonly in limited circumstances.\n\nThis bill aims to remedy this problem, which has stymied the walkability\nand bike-friendliness of communities in Upstate New York and on Long\nIsland, by creating an incentive for municipalities to construct these\nfeatures, which can sometimes be prohibitively expensive. By reducing\nthe project cost responsibility of municipalities which agree to incor-\nporate complete street features to half of its current level, incorpo-\nrating these features will become significantly more accessible to\ncommunities around New York to the benefit of the elderly, disabled, and\nsmall businesses that benefit from greater foot traffic.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2022: Underlying bill (S.3897) passed both houses and signed into law.\n\n2019-2020: Underlying bill S.4414-A Passed Senate / A.6454A Referred to\nTransportation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThe bill would increase the State's financial responsibility by an addi-\ntional two and one-half percent for eligible projects.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nIn the same manner as the underlying chapter, which was one year after\nbeing signed into law.\n",
